(function(){
"use strict";
window.STEP_READING = [
        ["Universities around the world are rethinking the traditional lecture. In a lecture, one person speaks while hundreds listen, and research shows that attention drops sharply after about twenty minutes. Some institutions now ask students to watch recorded material at home and use class time for discussion and problem solving. Early results are encouraging, although the approach demands far more preparation from both students and staff.",
          [["What does research say about attention in lectures?",["It falls sharply after roughly twenty minutes.","It stays constant for the whole hour.","It improves as the lecture continues.","It depends only on the subject."],0],
           ["What do some universities now do with class time?",["Use it for discussion and problem solving.","Use it for longer lectures.","Cancel it entirely.","Use it for examinations only."],0],
           ["What is the drawback the writer mentions?",["It requires much more preparation.","It costs nothing.","Students learn less.","It cannot be recorded."],0]]],
        ["Sleep is not simply a pause in activity. While we sleep, the brain sorts through the day's experiences, strengthening what matters and discarding what does not. Studies of adolescents show that those who sleep fewer than seven hours perform measurably worse on tasks requiring memory and judgement, even when they report feeling fine. Feeling alert, it seems, is a poor guide to actually being alert.",
          [["What does the brain do during sleep?",["It sorts experiences, keeping some and discarding others.","It stops working completely.","It only rests the body.","It creates new experiences."],0],
           ["What did studies of adolescents find?",["Those sleeping under seven hours did worse on memory tasks.","Sleep had no measurable effect.","Short sleepers performed better.","Only adults were affected."],0],
           ["What is the writer's final point?",["Feeling alert does not mean being alert.","Everyone needs the same amount of sleep.","Sleep is a waste of time.","Judgement is unaffected by sleep."],0]]],
        ["When a company hires, it is rarely looking only for technical ability. Employers repeatedly report that candidates fail not because they cannot do the work, but because they cannot explain it, accept criticism, or work with people who disagree with them. These abilities are harder to measure than a certificate, which is one reason interviews remain stressful and imperfect.",
          [["Why do candidates often fail, according to employers?",["They lack communication and teamwork skills.","They have no certificates.","They arrive late.","They ask for too much money."],0],
           ["Why are these abilities hard to assess?",["They are harder to measure than certificates.","They are unimportant.","They can be tested easily.","Employers ignore them."],0],
           ["What does the writer say about interviews?",["They remain stressful and imperfect.","They are perfectly reliable.","They are no longer used.","They only test technical skill."],0]]],
        ["Fresh water makes up less than three per cent of the water on Earth, and most of that is locked in ice. As populations grow, several regions now use water faster than rainfall can replace it. Engineers can remove salt from seawater, but the process consumes large amounts of energy and produces concentrated brine that must be disposed of carefully.",
          [["How much of Earth's water is fresh?",["Less than three per cent.","About half.","Nearly all of it.","Exactly ten per cent."],0],
           ["What problem do several regions face?",["They use water faster than rainfall replaces it.","They have too much rain.","They have no rivers at all.","They export all their water."],0],
           ["What is a disadvantage of removing salt from seawater?",["It uses much energy and creates brine.","It is completely free.","It produces drinking water instantly.","It has no known drawbacks."],0]]],
        ["Reading a graph is a skill, not a talent. A line that rises steeply may look dramatic, yet the impression depends entirely on the scale chosen for the vertical axis. Newspapers sometimes begin that axis well above zero, which exaggerates small differences. Careful readers therefore check the axes before they trust the shape.",
          [["What does the writer say about reading graphs?",["It is a skill that can be learned.","It is a natural talent.","It cannot be taught.","It is unnecessary."],0],
           ["What can exaggerate small differences?",["Starting the vertical axis above zero.","Using a very long horizontal axis.","Printing in colour.","Adding a title."],0],
           ["What do careful readers do first?",["Check the axes.","Believe the shape.","Ignore the numbers.","Look at the headline only."],0]]],
        ["Volunteering is often described as an act of pure generosity, but the evidence is more interesting than that. People who volunteer regularly report higher life satisfaction, a wider social network, and, in several long-term studies, better physical health. None of this makes the act less generous; it simply means that giving time is rarely a one-way transaction.",
          [["How is volunteering usually described?",["As an act of pure generosity.","As a paid profession.","As a waste of time.","As a legal duty."],0],
           ["What do regular volunteers report?",["Higher satisfaction, wider networks and better health.","Less free time only.","No change at all.","Lower income."],0],
           ["What is the writer's conclusion?",["Giving time is rarely a one-way transaction.","Volunteering is not generous.","Only the receiver benefits.","Studies are unreliable."],0]]],
        ["Learning a second language as an adult is often said to be hopeless. In fact, adults acquire grammar and vocabulary faster than children in the early stages, because they can use explicit rules and prior knowledge. Where children hold a clear advantage is pronunciation. The popular claim that adults simply cannot learn is therefore too broad to be useful.",
          [["What advantage do adults have early on?",["They learn grammar and vocabulary faster.","They have more free time.","They pronounce words perfectly.","They need no practice."],0],
           ["Where do children have the advantage?",["In pronunciation.","In grammar rules.","In vocabulary size.","In reading speed."],0],
           ["What does the writer think of the popular claim?",["It is too broad to be useful.","It is entirely correct.","It applies only to children.","It has been proven."],0]]],
        ["A common error in reasoning is to treat a correlation as a cause. Ice-cream sales and drowning rates rise together every summer, yet no one seriously believes that ice cream causes drowning; warm weather drives both. The lesson is not that correlations are worthless, but that they raise a question rather than answer one.",
          [["What common error does the writer describe?",["Treating a correlation as a cause.","Ignoring all statistics.","Using too many graphs.","Trusting experiments."],0],
           ["Why do ice-cream sales and drowning rise together?",["Warm weather causes both.","Ice cream causes drowning.","Drowning increases sales.","It is a coincidence with no cause."],0],
           ["What does the writer conclude about correlations?",["They raise a question rather than answer one.","They are worthless.","They always prove cause.","They should be ignored."],0]]],
        ["Cities are experimenting with car-free centres. Early objections focused on shops, which feared losing customers who arrive by car. Where the change has lasted several years, however, retail activity has usually risen: more people walk past a window than drive past it. The difficulty is the transition period, when the costs are immediate and the benefits are not.",
          [["What did shops fear?",["Losing customers who arrive by car.","Higher taxes.","Fewer deliveries by rail.","Longer opening hours."],0],
           ["What has usually happened after several years?",["Retail activity has risen.","Shops have closed.","Nothing measurable changed.","Traffic increased."],0],
           ["What is the main difficulty?",["Costs come immediately while benefits come later.","The change is illegal.","Nobody supports it.","It is too cheap."],0]]],
        ["An experiment is only as good as its control. If a new teaching method is tested on volunteers who are already motivated, any improvement may come from the volunteers rather than the method. Good studies therefore assign participants at random, so that the two groups differ, as far as possible, only in the treatment they receive.",
          [["What is the problem with testing on volunteers?",["Improvement may come from their motivation, not the method.","Volunteers are hard to find.","Volunteers are dishonest.","Volunteers learn nothing."],0],
           ["What do good studies do?",["Assign participants at random.","Choose the best students.","Use no control group.","Test only once."],0],
           ["What should distinguish the two groups?",["Only the treatment they receive.","Their age and income.","Their motivation.","Their school."],0]]],
        ["Saudi Arabia has invested heavily in solar energy, and the reason is geographic as much as economic. The country receives among the highest levels of solar radiation in the world, and its large open areas suit the very large installations that solar power requires. The remaining challenge is storage: the sun sets, but demand does not.",
          [["Why is solar energy suitable there?",["High solar radiation and large open areas.","Frequent rain.","A cool climate.","A small land area."],0],
           ["What does solar power require?",["Very large installations.","Very little space.","Constant rainfall.","Deep mines."],0],
           ["What is the remaining challenge?",["Storing energy for when the sun sets.","Finding sunlight.","Reducing land area.","Training pilots."],0]]],
        ["Museums face an awkward question about objects taken during the colonial period. Some argue that returning them would empty great collections and that the objects are better preserved where they are. Others reply that preservation was never the reason they were taken, and that a museum which keeps an object against the wishes of its country of origin is making a political choice, not a neutral one.",
          [["What is the argument for keeping the objects?",["Collections would be emptied and objects are well preserved.","They have no value.","Nobody wants them back.","They are too heavy to move."],0],
           ["How do the others reply?",["Preservation was not the original reason for taking them.","Preservation is impossible.","Museums should close.","The objects are fakes."],0],
           ["What does the writer say a museum's choice is?",["Political rather than neutral.","Purely scientific.","Always correct.","Made by the public."],0]]],


/* ═══ قطعٌ طويلةٌ بنمطِ ستيبَ الحقيقيّ ═══════════════════════════════
   شكوى الأب (٢٣ أكتوبر ٢٠٢٦): «قراءة القطعة بالنسبة لفهد مكرّرة وقصيرة».
   وقياسُه يصدّقها ويزيد: اثنتان وثلاثون جلسةَ قراءةٍ على اثنتَي عشرةَ
   قطعةً فقط — عشرٌ منها تكرّرت، واحدةٌ خمسَ مرّاتٍ وثلاثٌ أربعًا. وطولُ
   قطعِه ٥٣–٦٧ كلمةً، وهو ثالثُ ثانويٍّ يستعدّ لستيب.

   فعشرون قطعةً جديدةً ١٥٥–١٦٩ كلمةً (وسط ١٦٤)، لكلٍّ أربعةُ أسئلةٍ لا
   ثلاثة، بأربعةِ خياراتٍ كما في القطعِ السابقة. ومواضيعُها قُوبِلت
   باثنتَي عشرتِه وبقطعِ حسنٍ ورفيفَ قبل الكتابة، فلا موضوعَ يتكرّر.
   ونمطُها نمطُ الاختبار: حجّةٌ تُعرَض ثمّ يُسأل عن فكرتِها ونتيجتِها،
   لا معلومةٌ تُلتقَط من سطرٍ واحد. */
        ["A new treatment may appear to work simply because patients expect it to. This is the placebo effect, and it is not imaginary: measurable changes in pain, sleep and mood have been recorded in people given an inactive tablet. The effect is strongest where the symptom is subjective and the patient is hopeful.\n\nFor this reason a serious trial is blinded. Patients do not know whether they received the treatment or the placebo, and in a double-blind trial the staff do not know either, because a doctor who expects improvement tends to find it.\n\nBlinding is not always possible. A surgical technique cannot easily be disguised, and a drug with an obvious side effect may reveal itself. Researchers then use other controls and report the limitation openly.\n\nThe point is not that belief is powerful, though it is. The point is that a result which has not been separated from expectation is not yet evidence.",[["What does the passage say about the placebo effect?",["It produces changes that can be measured.","It exists only in the imagination.","It affects every symptom equally.","It has never been recorded."],0],["Why do staff as well as patients remain unaware in a double-blind trial?",["A doctor expecting improvement tends to find it.","Staff cannot be trusted with medicines.","It reduces the cost of the trial.","Patients prefer not to meet the staff."],0],["When is blinding difficult?",["When the treatment is surgical or has an obvious side effect.","When the illness is common.","When the patients are hopeful.","When the trial is small."],0],["What is the writer's main conclusion?",["A result not separated from expectation is not yet evidence.","Belief is more useful than medicine.","Trials should avoid placebos.","Subjective symptoms cannot be studied."],0]]],
        ["Most city centres offer parking either free or far below its real cost. The result is familiar: drivers circle the same streets searching for a space, adding traffic that was never going anywhere. Studies in several cities have found that a large share of downtown traffic at busy hours consists of cars simply looking for somewhere to stop.\n\nEconomists have long argued that the price should rise until roughly one space in seven stays empty. At that point a driver can expect to find a place quickly, the circling stops, and the street moves.\n\nThe proposal is unpopular, and understandably so: it looks like charging people for something they used to get free. Cities that have tried it found the objection softened when the money was visibly returned to the same neighbourhood, paying for pavements, lighting and cleaning.\n\nThe lesson is less about parking than about scarcity. A resource given away at no price is not free; it is simply paid for in time.",[["What does the passage identify as a large share of downtown traffic?",["Cars searching for a parking space.","Delivery lorries.","Public buses.","Cars leaving the city."],0],["What target do economists suggest?",["Raising the price until about one space in seven is empty.","Removing all parking spaces.","Making parking free at all hours.","Building car parks outside the city."],0],["What reduced opposition in cities that tried it?",["Spending the money visibly in the same neighbourhood.","Lowering the price again after a month.","Fining drivers who complained.","Keeping the policy secret."],0],["What wider point does the writer draw?",["A resource given away at no price is paid for in time.","Parking should always be free.","Cities should ban private cars.","Economists rarely understand cities."],0]]],
        ["During the Second World War, analysts examined aircraft returning from missions and mapped where the bullet holes clustered. The obvious recommendation was to add armour where the damage was heaviest. A statistician pointed out that the recommendation was exactly backwards.\n\nThe aircraft being examined were the ones that had come home. Damage in those places was evidently survivable. The areas with few holes on returning planes — the engines, the cockpit — were the areas where a hit meant the aircraft never returned at all. Armour belonged there.\n\nThe error has a name: survivorship bias. It appears whenever a sample is drawn only from those who made it through. Studies of successful companies that ignore the identical strategies of companies that failed, or advice collected only from people who finished a difficult course, repeat the same mistake in gentler form.\n\nThe remedy is uncomfortable but simple. Before reading any pattern in a sample, ask what the sample excluded, and whether the exclusion was random.",[["What did the analysts originally recommend?",["Adding armour where the returning planes were most damaged.","Flying at a higher altitude.","Reducing the weight of the aircraft.","Sending fewer missions."],0],["Why was that recommendation wrong?",["The damage on returning planes was survivable damage.","The holes had been counted incorrectly.","Armour was too expensive.","The planes were too old to repair."],0],["What does survivorship bias describe?",["Drawing conclusions from a sample of those who made it through.","A preference for older evidence.","An error in arithmetic.","A fear of statistics."],0],["What remedy does the writer suggest?",["Asking what a sample excluded and whether the exclusion was random.","Collecting larger samples only.","Trusting experienced analysts.","Avoiding statistics altogether."],0]]],
        ["Before a scientific paper is published, it is usually sent to several researchers working in the same area, who read it and advise the journal whether it should appear. They are not paid, they are often anonymous, and they may demand further experiments before they agree.\n\nThe system catches a great deal. Reviewers notice missing controls, overstated conclusions and statistics that do not support the claim. Papers frequently improve substantially between submission and publication.\n\nIt also fails in predictable ways. Reviewers are rarely able to detect deliberate fraud, since they see the written account rather than the laboratory. They are slow, sometimes taking many months. And because they are specialists in the same field, they may share its assumptions, so an idea that challenges those assumptions can face a harder passage than it deserves.\n\nNone of this makes review worthless. It makes it a filter rather than a guarantee — and a reader who treats publication as proof has misunderstood what the filter does.",[["Who reviews a paper before publication?",["Researchers working in the same area.","The journal's advertisers.","The general public.","The author's students."],0],["What do reviewers commonly catch?",["Missing controls and overstated conclusions.","Spelling mistakes only.","Deliberate fraud in the laboratory.","Errors in the printing."],0],["Why may a challenging idea face difficulty?",["Reviewers in the same field may share its assumptions.","Journals refuse all new ideas.","Reviewers are paid to reject papers.","New ideas are always wrong."],0],["How does the writer describe peer review?",["A filter rather than a guarantee.","A guarantee of truth.","A waste of time.","A legal requirement."],0]]],
        ["When a service is offered free, the cost has not disappeared; it has moved. Someone is paying, and understanding who changes how the service behaves.\n\nIn the common arrangement, attention is sold. The service is free to the reader because advertisers pay for the reader's time, which means the service is designed to hold that time rather than to end the visit quickly. A tool that helped you finish and leave would be, in that arrangement, a worse product.\n\nOther arrangements exist. Subscription services are paid by the person using them, and therefore have reason to respect that person's time. Publicly funded services answer to a different pressure again, neither advertising nor subscription.\n\nNone of these is simply good or bad. The useful habit is to ask, of any service that costs nothing, a single question: whose behaviour is this designed to change, and who paid for the change? The answer usually explains the design better than the advertised purpose does.",[["What does the writer say happens to the cost of a free service?",["It moves to someone else rather than disappearing.","It is removed entirely.","It is paid by the government in every case.","It is added to the price later."],0],["Why might an advertising-funded tool avoid helping you leave quickly?",["Its income depends on holding your time.","Its staff are poorly trained.","Leaving quickly is technically impossible.","Readers prefer longer visits."],0],["What reason do subscription services have to respect your time?",["The person using the service is the one paying.","Advertisers demand it.","The law requires it.","They have fewer users."],0],["What question does the writer recommend asking?",["Whose behaviour the service is designed to change, and who paid.","How many people use the service.","When the service was founded.","Whether the service is popular."],0]]],
        ["A fishing ground that belongs to everyone belongs, in practice, to no one. Each boat gains the full value of the fish it lands, while the cost of a thinner stock is shared by the whole fleet. The arithmetic facing any single skipper therefore favours fishing harder, even when every skipper understands that the stock is shrinking.\n\nThis pattern is called the tragedy of the commons, and it is not a story about greed. It can occur among people who are entirely reasonable and fully informed, because the incentive each one faces points the wrong way.\n\nWhat breaks the pattern is a rule that changes the arithmetic. Quotas that give each boat a fixed share mean that a skipper who protects the stock keeps part of the benefit. Enforcement matters as much as the rule, since a quota nobody checks is an invitation.\n\nThe lesson travels well beyond fishing: shared grazing, shared aquifers, shared bandwidth. Wherever benefits are private and costs are shared, the arithmetic needs rewriting.",[["Why does the arithmetic favour fishing harder?",["The gain is private while the cost of a thinner stock is shared.","Fish are easier to catch when stocks fall.","Skippers do not understand the problem.","Fuel is cheaper at sea."],0],["What does the writer say about greed?",["The pattern can occur among reasonable, well-informed people.","Greed is the only cause.","Greed is impossible to measure.","Greed affects only large fleets."],0],["What makes a quota effective?",["Enforcement, so that the rule is actually checked.","Keeping it secret from the fleet.","Setting it as high as possible.","Changing it every month."],0],["Where else does the writer say the pattern appears?",["Shared grazing, aquifers and bandwidth.","Only in deep-sea fishing.","Only in private businesses.","Only in poor countries."],0]]],
        ["Insurance looks like a bet, and in one sense it is. You pay a sum you can afford in order to avoid a loss you could not. The company accepts the opposite side of that bargain many thousands of times.\n\nWhat makes the arrangement work is not luck but number. A single house fire is unpredictable; the proportion of houses that burn in a large city over a year is remarkably stable. The company is not guessing about your house. It is relying on the steadiness of a large total.\n\nTwo problems follow. People who know they are at high risk are the keenest to buy, which raises the average claim; and people who are insured may take less care, which raises it further. Insurers answer with medical questions, excesses that leave part of the loss with the customer, and premiums that rise after a claim.\n\nNone of these devices is popular. All of them exist because the pool only works if what enters it stays representative.",[["What makes insurance work, according to the passage?",["The steadiness of proportions across a large number of cases.","The good luck of the company.","The honesty of every customer.","Government support."],0],["Why does the writer say the company is not guessing about your house?",["It relies on the stable proportion of houses that burn in a city.","It inspects every house personally.","It refuses risky houses.","It insures only new houses."],0],["What are the two problems mentioned?",["High-risk people buy most, and the insured may take less care.","Premiums are too low and claims are too slow.","Companies are too large and customers too few.","Fires are rare and floods are common."],0],["Why do insurers use excesses and medical questions?",["To keep what enters the pool representative.","To make the paperwork longer.","To please the regulator.","To reduce their own profits."],0]]],
        ["In 2015 a team attempted to repeat one hundred published psychology experiments. Fewer than half produced the original result. The finding was uncomfortable, and the discussion that followed has been more valuable than the number itself.\n\nSeveral causes were identified. Small samples produce unstable results, and a striking finding from twenty participants may simply be noise. Journals preferred surprising positive results, so studies that found nothing were rarely published and rarely counted. And researchers, without intending to deceive, could test many variations and report the one that worked.\n\nThe responses have been practical rather than dramatic. Many researchers now register their hypothesis and analysis plan before collecting data, which makes a later change visible. Journals have begun accepting papers on the strength of the method, before the result is known. Sample sizes have grown.\n\nThe episode is often reported as a scandal. It is better read as a field discovering a weakness in public and correcting it — which is precisely what a scientific field is supposed to do.",[["What happened when one hundred experiments were repeated?",["Fewer than half produced the original result.","All of them were confirmed.","None of them could be tested.","The results were never published."],0],["Why were studies finding nothing rarely counted?",["Journals preferred surprising positive results.","They were technically impossible.","They cost more to run.","Researchers were forbidden to write them."],0],["What does registering a plan before collecting data achieve?",["It makes a later change to the analysis visible.","It guarantees a positive result.","It shortens the experiment.","It removes the need for a control."],0],["How does the writer prefer the episode to be read?",["As a field discovering a weakness publicly and correcting it.","As proof that psychology is worthless.","As an isolated scandal.","As a failure of journalism."],0]]],
        ["Before the 1950s, loading a ship was slow, skilled and expensive work. Cargo arrived in barrels, sacks and crates of every shape, and dockers packed them by hand like a puzzle. A ship could spend longer in port than at sea.\n\nThe container changed the arithmetic rather than the technology. A steel box of agreed dimensions could be filled at a factory, sealed, lifted by crane, carried by lorry, stacked on a ship and opened only at its destination. Handling cost fell so far that distance stopped being the main component of price.\n\nThe consequences reached well beyond shipping. Factories could be sited wherever production was cheapest rather than wherever the customers were, since moving the goods had become almost incidental. Ports that could not take container cranes declined; others that could, and had deep water, grew into cities.\n\nIt is a useful reminder that the inventions which reorganise the world are not always the clever ones. Sometimes they are merely the ones everybody agrees to use.",[["What was loading like before the 1950s?",["Slow, skilled and expensive hand work.","Fully automatic.","Done only at night.","Carried out by machines on shore."],0],["What did the container mainly change?",["The arithmetic of handling cost.","The speed of the ships themselves.","The design of factories' machinery.","The training of sailors."],0],["What became possible for factories?",["Siting them wherever production was cheapest.","Loading their own ships.","Building their own ports.","Avoiding lorries entirely."],0],["What is the writer's closing point?",["World-changing inventions are sometimes simply the agreed ones.","Cleverness always wins.","Ports should not expand.","Shipping is no longer important."],0]]],
        ["Living carbon contains a small, steady proportion of an unstable form of the element. While an organism is alive it keeps taking that form in from its surroundings, so the proportion inside it stays near the proportion outside. When the organism dies the intake stops, and the unstable carbon begins to decay at a rate that does not vary.\n\nMeasuring what remains therefore gives an age. Because the decay is steady, the proportion left behind is a clock that started at the moment of death. The method works well for samples up to roughly fifty thousand years old; beyond that too little remains to measure.\n\nThe technique requires care. The proportion in the atmosphere has not been perfectly constant, so raw results are corrected against tree rings and other records of known age. Contamination by newer carbon will make a sample appear younger than it is.\n\nUsed carelessly it produces confident nonsense. Used properly it has rewritten the dates of human settlement across whole continents.",[["Why does the proportion stay steady while an organism is alive?",["It keeps taking the unstable form in from its surroundings.","Decay stops during life.","The organism produces it.","The proportion outside also stops changing."],0],["What starts the clock?",["The moment the organism dies and intake stops.","The moment the sample is found.","The first measurement.","The burial of the sample."],0],["Why are raw results corrected against tree rings?",["The atmospheric proportion has not been perfectly constant.","Tree rings decay at the same rate.","Trees are easier to date.","It makes the samples older."],0],["What does contamination by newer carbon do?",["It makes a sample appear younger than it is.","It makes a sample appear older.","It has no effect.","It destroys the sample."],0]]],
        ["Translation is often imagined as substitution: find the word in the other language and put it in place. Anyone who has tried knows the picture is wrong. Words carry a history of use that rarely matches across languages, and a sentence carries a weight that its words do not state.\n\nSome difficulties are structural. A language that marks politeness in its verbs forces the translator to choose a level of formality that the original never had to declare. A language with no definite article obliges a decision about which nouns become 'the'.\n\nOthers are cultural. A proverb translated literally may be perfectly clear and entirely dead, because its force came from being familiar rather than from its image.\n\nThe translator is therefore not copying but deciding, repeatedly, what to preserve: the sound, the rhythm, the literal sense, the effect on a reader who shares none of the original assumptions. Something is always surrendered. The skill lies in choosing what, and in knowing that a choice was made.",[["What picture of translation does the writer reject?",["That it is a matter of substituting words.","That it requires two languages.","That it takes time.","That it can be taught."],0],["What does a language marking politeness in its verbs force?",["A choice of formality the original never declared.","A change of tense.","The removal of pronouns.","A shorter sentence."],0],["Why can a literally translated proverb fail?",["Its force came from familiarity rather than its image.","Its words have no equivalents.","It is always too long.","Proverbs cannot be written down."],0],["What does the writer say the skill of translation lies in?",["Choosing what to surrender and knowing a choice was made.","Preserving everything at once.","Avoiding all cultural material.","Working as quickly as possible."],0]]],
        ["For most of human history the night sky was part of ordinary experience. Today roughly a third of people on Earth cannot see the Milky Way from where they live, not because it has moved but because the sky above them is brighter than it is.\n\nThe cause is light sent upward or sideways rather than down. A lamp that illuminates a street is doing its job; the same lamp, unshielded, also illuminates the air above it, and scattered light fills the sky with a faint glow that drowns the fainter stars.\n\nThe consequences are not only aesthetic. Migrating birds navigate by the sky and are drawn off course by lit towers. Insects gather and die around lamps. Observatories have been pushed to ever more remote mountains.\n\nUnusually among environmental problems, this one is reversible within a night. Shielding a lamp so its light goes downward costs little, improves the lighting it was installed for, and the sky returns the moment the glare stops.",[["Why can a third of people not see the Milky Way?",["The sky above them is brighter than it is.","The Milky Way has moved.","Their eyesight has weakened.","Clouds have increased."],0],["What causes the glow?",["Light sent upward or sideways and scattered in the air.","Light pointed at the ground.","Heat from the lamps.","Smoke from traffic."],0],["What happens to migrating birds?",["They are drawn off course by lit towers.","They navigate more accurately.","They stop migrating entirely.","They avoid cities completely."],0],["Why does the writer call this problem unusual?",["It is reversible within a night and shielding costs little.","It cannot be solved at any price.","It affects only astronomers.","It has no known cause."],0]]],
        ["A language is not lost when its last speaker dies. It is lost much earlier, at the moment parents stop using it with their children, because from then on the number of speakers can only fall.\n\nThat decision is seldom made freely. It is usually a response to a judgement, often taught in school, that the home language is an obstacle. Parents who want their children to prosper act on the judgement, and the change takes one generation.\n\nWhat disappears with a language is not only vocabulary. Oral literature goes, and so do classifications built over centuries — the names of plants, winds, soils and seasons that a community needed and recorded in the only place it had.\n\nThe usual reply is that fewer languages make communication easier. The reply misunderstands the alternative. Children who keep the home language and learn the wider one as well end up with both, and the evidence that this harms them has never materialised.",[["When is a language effectively lost?",["When parents stop using it with their children.","When its last speaker dies.","When it stops being written.","When it is not taught at university."],0],["What usually drives that decision?",["A judgement that the home language is an obstacle.","A government ban.","A shortage of books.","The difficulty of the grammar."],0],["What else disappears besides vocabulary?",["Oral literature and classifications of plants, winds and soils.","Only the written alphabet.","The community's buildings.","Nothing of importance."],0],["How does the writer answer the argument for fewer languages?",["Children can keep the home language and learn the wider one too.","Communication is unimportant.","Wider languages should be banned.","The argument cannot be answered."],0]]],
        ["Weather is what the atmosphere is doing now; climate is the distribution of what it does over decades. The distinction sounds pedantic until it is used, at which point it does most of the work in the argument.\n\nA cold week says nothing about climate, in the same way that one tall pupil says nothing about the average height of a school. Nor does a single heatwave prove anything by itself. What can be said is that the distribution has shifted, so events that were once rare have become less rare — and the clearest evidence is not any single event but the changing frequency of extremes.\n\nThis is why attribution studies ask an unusual question. They do not ask whether a particular storm was caused by a warming atmosphere, which is unanswerable. They ask how much more likely a storm of that severity has become.\n\nIt is a less satisfying question than the one people want answered, and it is the one that can be answered honestly.",[["How does the writer define climate?",["The distribution of atmospheric behaviour over decades.","The weather of a single season.","The temperature at noon.","The forecast for next week."],0],["What comparison is used for a cold week?",["One tall pupil and a school's average height.","A single coin toss and a fair coin.","One book and a library.","A short road and a long journey."],0],["What does the writer call the clearest evidence?",["The changing frequency of extreme events.","A single record temperature.","The length of the summer.","The number of storms in one year."],0],["What question do attribution studies ask?",["How much more likely an event of that severity has become.","Whether a storm was caused by warming.","When the next storm will arrive.","Which country is responsible."],0]]],
        ["A queue that moves steadily feels shorter than a queue of the same length that stops and starts, even when the wait is identical. Research on waiting has produced a set of findings that are consistent enough to design around.\n\nUnoccupied time feels longer than occupied time, which is why mirrors near lifts and information screens in stations reduce complaints without reducing the wait. Uncertain waits feel longer than known ones: a sign saying the next train arrives in nine minutes is more tolerable than no sign at all, even when nine minutes is the longer wait. Unexplained waits feel longest of all, and an announcement giving a reason reliably lowers irritation.\n\nAnd people tolerate a long wait for something they value far better than a short wait for something they do not.\n\nNone of this shortens anything. It is a reminder that the experience being managed is not the duration but the perception of it — and that the two can be improved separately.",[["What feels shorter than a stopping queue of equal length?",["A queue that moves steadily.","A longer queue.","A queue with no sign.","A queue indoors."],0],["Why do information screens reduce complaints?",["Occupied time feels shorter than unoccupied time.","They make the train arrive sooner.","They shorten the queue.","They entertain the staff."],0],["What kind of wait feels longest?",["An unexplained wait.","A short wait.","A wait with a sign.","A wait for something valued."],0],["What is the writer's conclusion?",["Duration and the perception of it can be improved separately.","Queues cannot be improved.","Signs make waits longer.","Perception does not matter."],0]]],
        ["Much of the water beneath the Arabian Peninsula fell as rain thousands of years ago, when the climate was far wetter than it is now. It has been sitting in deep aquifers ever since, and at present rates of rainfall it is not being replaced in any meaningful sense.\n\nThis is why hydrologists call it fossil water and treat it less like a river than like a mineral deposit. A river can be used indefinitely if the rate of use stays below the rate of flow. A deposit can only be spent.\n\nThe distinction matters because the two are managed differently. For a renewable source the question is what rate is sustainable. For a finite one the question is what the water is being spent on, and what will be in place when it runs low.\n\nAgriculture has historically taken the largest share. The policy debate is therefore less about finding more water than about deciding which uses are worth a resource that does not return.",[["Why is the water described as fossil water?",["It fell as rain thousands of years ago and is not being replaced.","It is found beside fossils.","It is unusually old in taste.","It lies under rock rather than sand."],0],["What comparison does the writer use?",["A mineral deposit rather than a river.","A lake rather than a well.","A cloud rather than a sea.","A canal rather than a spring."],0],["What is the key question for a finite source?",["What the water is being spent on, and what comes next.","What rate of use is sustainable forever.","How deep the aquifer lies.","How quickly rain falls."],0],["Which use has historically taken the largest share?",["Agriculture.","Industry.","Household washing.","Tourism."],0]]],
        ["A standardised test is valued because it is comparable: the same questions, the same time, the same marking for everyone. That comparability is real, and it is often the fairest instrument available when places are scarce.\n\nIt also narrows what counts. A test measures what can be asked in the format and the time, which means it measures some abilities well, others poorly, and several not at all. Persistence over months, the ability to work with people who disagree, the judgement to notice that a question is the wrong question — none of these fit inside two hours.\n\nThe serious risk is not inaccuracy but displacement. When a score decides admission, teaching reorganises itself around the score, and the narrow set becomes the taught set. The test then measures itself.\n\nThe defensible position is modest: use the score as one piece of evidence, state plainly what it does not cover, and resist the temptation to treat a convenient number as a complete one.",[["Why is a standardised test valued?",["It is comparable across everyone who takes it.","It is enjoyable to sit.","It is cheap to write.","It measures every ability."],0],["What does the writer say a test necessarily does?",["It narrows what counts to what fits the format and time.","It removes all unfairness.","It replaces teaching.","It measures persistence well."],0],["What is described as the serious risk?",["Teaching reorganising itself around the score.","Marking errors.","Tests being too long.","Students arriving late."],0],["What position does the writer defend?",["Using the score as one piece of evidence and stating its limits.","Abolishing all testing.","Trusting the score completely.","Replacing tests with interviews."],0]]],
        ["Tall buildings were technically possible long before they were built. Stone and brick could be piled higher than anyone needed; what stopped them was not the walls but the stairs. Above about six floors, the upper storeys were cheap, undesirable and let to whoever could not afford better.\n\nTwo changes reversed this. The safety brake made a passenger lift something people would willingly enter, and the steel frame moved the load off the walls, so that the lower floors did not have to be solid masonry to hold the upper ones.\n\nThe effect on value was immediate and inverted. The top floor, formerly the worst address in the building, became the best, because it had the view and the quiet and now cost nothing extra to reach.\n\nIt is a clean example of a pattern worth watching. A technology does not only make something possible. It frequently rearranges what people want, and the rearrangement outlasts the novelty.",[["What limited the height of early buildings?",["The stairs rather than the walls.","The strength of stone.","Local law.","A shortage of workers."],0],["What did the steel frame change?",["It moved the load off the walls.","It made lifts unnecessary.","It reduced the number of floors.","It replaced the roof."],0],["What happened to the value of the top floor?",["It became the best address rather than the worst.","It stayed the cheapest.","It was closed to tenants.","It was used only for storage."],0],["What wider pattern does the writer draw?",["A technology rearranges what people want, not only what is possible.","Old buildings are better built.","Height is always desirable.","Novelty never lasts."],0]]],
        ["Switching from one task to another is not free, and the cost is not only the seconds spent moving between them. Part of the attention stays behind. Having stopped a report to answer a message, a worker returns to the report carrying a fragment of the message, and the first minutes back are spent reconstructing where the thought had reached.\n\nResearchers call this attention residue, and it explains a familiar complaint: a day can be entirely occupied and produce almost nothing, because no interval was long enough for the reconstruction to finish before the next interruption.\n\nThe effect grows with the difficulty of the task. Sorting post survives interruption easily; writing an argument does not, because the structure being held in mind collapses and must be rebuilt.\n\nThe practical response is unglamorous. Group similar work together, protect a small number of long intervals rather than distributing attention evenly, and treat an unbroken hour as the scarce resource it actually is.",[["What does the writer say about the cost of switching tasks?",["Part of the attention stays with the previous task.","It is only the seconds spent moving.","There is no cost at all.","It falls as the day goes on."],0],["What familiar complaint does attention residue explain?",["A fully occupied day that produces almost nothing.","Waking up tired.","Disliking one's colleagues.","Forgetting appointments."],0],["Which kind of work suffers most?",["Work holding a structure in mind, such as writing an argument.","Sorting post.","Simple repeated tasks.","Work done in company."],0],["What practical response is recommended?",["Grouping similar work and protecting a few long intervals.","Working faster between interruptions.","Answering messages immediately.","Spreading attention evenly."],0]]],
        ["When a buyer cannot judge quality before purchase, the market has a problem. The seller who knows the goods are poor has every reason to claim otherwise, and because the claim costs nothing it carries no information. If buyers expect this, they offer only what poor goods are worth, and good sellers withdraw.\n\nThe way out is a signal that would be expensive to imitate. A long guarantee is affordable for a manufacturer whose product rarely fails and ruinous for one whose product often does, so the guarantee says something the advertisement cannot.\n\nThe same logic explains much that looks wasteful. A qualification that takes four years signals persistence partly because it takes four years. A shop that invests in an expensive building is announcing that it expects to be there long enough to recover the cost.\n\nThe test of any claim, then, is not whether it is confident but whether making it falsely would have hurt. Talk that costs nothing is worth what it costs.",[["Why does a seller's claim carry no information?",["Making it costs nothing, so any seller can make it.","Buyers never listen to sellers.","Claims are forbidden by law.","Good sellers do not advertise."],0],["What makes a long guarantee informative?",["It would be ruinous for a seller whose product often fails.","It is printed in large letters.","It is required of every manufacturer.","It costs the buyer nothing."],0],["What does an expensive shop building announce?",["That the seller expects to stay long enough to recover the cost.","That the goods are imported.","That the owner dislikes saving.","That rents are low."],0],["What test does the writer propose for any claim?",["Whether making it falsely would have hurt the one who made it.","Whether it is stated confidently.","Whether it is repeated often.","Whether it is written down."],0]]],
        ["Bees do more than make honey. As they move between flowers they carry pollen, allowing plants to produce seeds and fruit. A large share of the crops people eat depends on this service, which no machine has yet replaced at scale. When bee numbers fall, farmers notice the cost before scientists finish explaining the cause.",[["What service do bees provide besides honey?",["They carry pollen between flowers.","They remove insects from crops.","They water the plants.","They improve the soil."],0],["Why is this service hard to replace?",["No machine has matched it at scale.","It costs nothing to copy.","Few crops depend on it.","Farmers do not value it."],0],["Who notices the effect of falling bee numbers first?",["Farmers, through cost.","Scientists, through experiments.","Tourists.","Honey sellers only."],0]]],
        ["The first public libraries were controversial. Opponents argued that giving books away would ruin booksellers and encourage idleness among workers. Supporters replied that a society which educates its poorest members grows wealthier, not poorer. Two centuries later the argument sounds strange, which is often what happens to objections that lose.",[["What did opponents of public libraries fear?",["Harm to booksellers and idleness among workers.","A shortage of paper.","Too few readers.","Higher taxes on books."],0],["What did supporters argue?",["Educating the poorest makes society wealthier.","Books should cost more.","Libraries should be private.","Reading is a luxury."],0],["What does the writer say about the old objections?",["They now sound strange, as losing arguments often do.","They were proved correct.","They are still widely held.","They were never written down."],0]]],
        ["A map is always a simplification, and every simplification is a choice. The familiar world map stretches land near the poles, making some countries appear far larger than they are. Other projections preserve area but distort shape. There is no perfect flat picture of a round planet, only trade-offs that the mapmaker selects and the reader rarely questions.",[["What does the familiar world map do to polar land?",["It stretches it, exaggerating size.","It shrinks it.","It removes it.","It shows it perfectly."],0],["What do other projections preserve?",["Area, at the cost of shape.","Both area and shape.","Neither area nor shape.","Only distance."],0],["What is the writer's main point?",["Every flat map involves trade-offs that readers seldom question.","Maps are useless.","One projection is clearly best.","Mapmakers never choose."],0]]],
        ["Noise is not merely an annoyance. Long-term exposure to traffic noise has been linked in several studies to raised blood pressure and disturbed sleep, even among people who insist they have stopped noticing it. The body, it appears, continues to respond to sounds that the conscious mind has learned to ignore.",[["What has traffic noise been linked to?",["Raised blood pressure and disturbed sleep.","Improved concentration.","Better hearing.","Lower heart rates."],0],["Who is affected, according to the studies?",["Even people who say they no longer notice it.","Only light sleepers.","Only children.","Nobody who lives in cities."],0],["What does the passage suggest about the body?",["It responds even when the mind ignores the sound.","It adapts completely.","It needs silence to function.","It is unaffected by noise."],0]]],
        ["Translating poetry is sometimes called impossible, and the complaint is reasonable. Sound, rhythm and association rarely survive the journey between languages. Yet readers who know only one language still meet Rumi and Homer, and they meet them through translators who accepted an imperfect task rather than refusing a necessary one.",[["Why is translating poetry called impossible?",["Sound, rhythm and association rarely survive.","Poems are too short.","Dictionaries are incomplete.","Poets forbid it."],0],["What do translators make possible?",["Readers of one language meeting foreign poets.","Perfect reproductions of poems.","The end of original writing.","Faster language learning."],0],["How does the writer describe the translator's choice?",["Accepting an imperfect but necessary task.","Refusing all compromise.","Rewriting the poem freely.","Avoiding difficult poets."],0]]],
        ["Coral reefs occupy less than one per cent of the ocean floor yet shelter roughly a quarter of all marine species. The corals themselves are animals living with algae that supply them with food. When water warms beyond a narrow range, the corals expel the algae and turn white, a process called bleaching. They can recover, but only if the heat passes quickly.",[["How much of the ocean floor do reefs occupy?",["Less than one per cent.","About a quarter.","Roughly half.","Nearly all of it."],0],["What relationship do corals have with algae?",["The algae supply the corals with food.","The corals eat the algae immediately.","They compete for space.","They never interact."],0],["When can bleached coral recover?",["If the heat passes quickly.","Only if the water cools for years.","Never.","Only in deep water."],0]]],
        ["The word 'deadline' once meant a line around a prison beyond which a prisoner would be shot. Its modern use, a date by which work must be finished, keeps something of the old menace. Language does this often: a term loses its original setting but carries a trace of feeling into contexts that have nothing to do with where it began.",[["What did 'deadline' originally mean?",["A line in a prison beyond which prisoners were shot.","A finishing date for work.","A printed boundary on a map.","A line in a newspaper."],0],["What does the modern use keep?",["A trace of the old menace.","The exact original meaning.","Nothing of its history.","Its connection to prisons."],0],["What general point does the writer make about language?",["Words carry feeling into new contexts after losing the old one.","Words never change meaning.","Old meanings are always clearer.","Slang replaces all old words."],0]]],
        ["Many people assume that creativity arrives suddenly, as a flash. Interviews with working artists and scientists suggest something duller and more encouraging: long periods of patient attention, frequent failure, and the habit of returning to a problem after sleep. The flash, when it comes, usually arrives to a mind that has already done the work.",[["What do many people assume about creativity?",["That it arrives suddenly as a flash.","That it cannot be studied.","That it is inherited.","That it requires wealth."],0],["What do interviews with artists and scientists suggest?",["Patient attention, failure and returning after sleep.","Instant inspiration with no effort.","That talent is fixed at birth.","That groups are always better."],0],["Why does the writer call this 'encouraging'?",["Because the process is work that can be done.","Because it requires no effort.","Because failure is avoidable.","Because sleep is unnecessary."],0]]],
        ["Street markets persist in cities that have every modern shop. Economists who study them point to something supermarkets cannot easily supply: prices that move during the day, sellers who recognise regular buyers, and goods sold in the small quantities that a single household actually needs.",[["What question do economists ask about street markets?",["Why they persist beside modern shops.","Why they charge more.","Why they close early.","Why they sell only food."],0],["What is one advantage mentioned?",["Prices that change during the day.","Fixed annual pricing.","Larger packages.","Longer queues."],0],["Who benefits from small quantities?",["A single household buying what it needs.","Large restaurants.","Export companies.","Supermarket chains."],0]]],
        ["A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, yet patients given one often improve. This is not imagination in the dismissive sense: measurable changes in pain reporting and even in some hormone levels have been recorded. The effect complicates every trial, which is why serious studies compare a new drug against a placebo rather than against nothing.",[["What is a placebo?",["A treatment with no active ingredient.","A new experimental drug.","A kind of surgery.","A dietary supplement."],0],["What evidence shows the effect is not mere imagination?",["Measurable changes in pain reporting and hormones.","Patients' written opinions only.","Doctors' impressions.","Longer hospital stays."],0],["Why do serious studies use a placebo group?",["Because the effect complicates results otherwise.","To save money.","To shorten the trial.","Because law forbids other methods."],0]]],
        ["Handwriting is disappearing from many classrooms, replaced by keyboards that are faster and easier to read. Some researchers object, pointing to studies in which students who wrote notes by hand remembered more than those who typed. The likely reason is not the pen itself but the speed: typing fast enough to copy removes the need to decide what matters.",[["Why are keyboards replacing handwriting?",["They are faster and easier to read.","They are cheaper to repair.","Students prefer the sound.","Teachers cannot read writing."],0],["What did the studies find?",["Students who wrote by hand remembered more.","Typing improved memory.","There was no difference.","Only spelling improved."],0],["What is the likely reason for the difference?",["Typing fast removes the need to select what matters.","Pens improve the brain directly.","Paper smells better.","Keyboards are distracting to look at."],0]]],
        ["In the ninth century, Baghdad's House of Wisdom gathered scholars who translated Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic. The project was not passive copying: translators corrected errors, added commentary, and preserved texts whose originals were later lost. Much of what Europe rediscovered centuries afterwards arrived by this route.",[["What did the House of Wisdom do?",["It translated works from several languages into Arabic.","It built observatories only.","It trained soldiers.","It printed newspapers."],0],["Why was the work not passive copying?",["Translators corrected errors and added commentary.","They copied word for word.","They worked without reading.","They shortened every text."],0],["What happened to some original texts?",["They were later lost, surviving only in translation.","They were all preserved in Greece.","They were never written down.","They were rewritten in Latin first."],0]]],
        ["Insurance works because misfortune is rare and unpredictable for an individual but regular and calculable for a crowd. No company can say which house will burn; it can say, with uncomfortable accuracy, how many will. This is why insurers need large numbers of customers and why very small schemes tend to fail.",[["What is unpredictable for an individual but calculable for a crowd?",["Misfortune.","Income.","Age.","Education."],0],["What can an insurer predict?",["How many houses will burn, not which one.","Which house will burn.","When a customer will move.","The price of building materials."],0],["Why do very small schemes tend to fail?",["They lack the large numbers the calculation needs.","They charge too little tax.","They have too many staff.","They insure only new houses."],0]]],
        ["Few inventions spread as quickly as the mobile phone, and few have been adopted so unevenly. In several countries people moved directly from having no telephone to carrying one, skipping the expensive stage of laying wires. The result was a network built in years rather than decades, though coverage still follows roads and cities more closely than need.",[["What stage did some countries skip?",["Laying telephone wires.","Building roads.","Training engineers.","Importing handsets."],0],["How quickly was the network built there?",["In years rather than decades.","Over two centuries.","In a single month.","It was never completed."],0],["What does coverage follow most closely?",["Roads and cities.","Areas of greatest need.","Rivers.","National borders."],0]]],
        ["Museums increasingly face a question their founders never considered: whether objects taken during empire should be returned. Defenders of large collections argue that they allow comparison across cultures in one place. Critics reply that the comparison is made possible by an absence elsewhere, and that the people most entitled to see the objects must travel furthest to do so.",[["What question do museums now face?",["Whether objects taken during empire should be returned.","Whether to charge entry fees.","Whether to open at night.","Whether to build new wings."],0],["What do defenders of large collections argue?",["They allow comparison across cultures in one place.","They earn the most money.","They are safer from theft.","They attract scholars only."],0],["What is the critics' reply?",["The comparison depends on an absence elsewhere.","Comparison is impossible.","Museums should close.","Objects should be sold."],0]]],
        ["A rumour spreads faster than a correction, and the reason is structural rather than moral. The rumour is new, surprising and easy to retell; the correction is late, dull and requires the listener to remember what was claimed. By the time the second message arrives, the first has already been repeated by people the listener trusts.",[["Why does a rumour spread faster than a correction?",["It is new, surprising and easy to retell.","People are dishonest.","Corrections are forbidden.","Rumours are always true."],0],["What does a correction demand of the listener?",["Remembering what was originally claimed.","Paying a fee.","Reading a long book.","Meeting the original speaker."],0],["Why is the first message hard to displace?",["Trusted people have already repeated it.","It is printed in newspapers.","It is shorter.","It is official."],0]]],
        ["Desert plants solve the same problem in different ways. Some grow deep roots to reach water far below; others spread shallow roots wide to capture brief rain before it evaporates. A few store water in thick stems and simply wait. Each strategy works, and none would work everywhere, which is why a single desert can hold all three.",[["What problem do desert plants share?",["Obtaining scarce water.","Avoiding strong wind.","Finding enough shade.","Escaping predators."],0],["What do shallow-rooted plants capture?",["Brief rain before it evaporates.","Deep groundwater.","Morning dew only.","Water stored by other plants."],0],["Why can one desert hold all three strategies?",["No single strategy works everywhere.","They never grow together.","Only one is successful.","The desert has three climates."],0]]],
        ["Ancient Roman concrete has survived two thousand years in seawater, while much modern concrete crumbles within decades. Analysis has shown that the Roman mixture contained volcanic ash which reacted with seawater to form new crystals inside the cracks. The material did not merely resist damage; it repaired itself, slowly, using the very water that attacked it.",[["How long has Roman concrete survived in seawater?",["About two thousand years.","About two hundred years.","Fifty years.","Five hundred years."],0],["What did the Roman mixture contain?",["Volcanic ash.","Modern cement.","Steel fibres.","Crushed glass."],0],["What unusual property did the material have?",["It repaired itself using seawater.","It floated.","It never cracked at all.","It became lighter with age."],0]]],
        ["Economists use the word 'cost' more widely than most speakers do. Choosing to study on Friday evening costs money only if you would otherwise have earned some, but it always costs whatever you would have done instead. This hidden price, the value of the best option refused, explains decisions that look irrational until it is counted.",[["How do economists use the word 'cost'?",["More widely than most speakers.","Only for money spent.","Only in business.","Never for time."],0],["What does studying on Friday evening always cost?",["Whatever you would have done instead.","A fixed sum of money.","Nothing at all.","Only your energy."],0],["What does this hidden price explain?",["Decisions that otherwise look irrational.","Why prices rise.","Why people save money.","Why studying is useless."],0]]],
        ["Migrating birds navigate by several systems at once: the position of the sun, patterns of polarised light, star maps learned as chicks, and a sense of the Earth's magnetic field. Experiments that disable one system rarely stop the journey, because another takes over. Redundancy, not any single brilliant instrument, is what makes the flight reliable.",[["How many navigation systems do migrating birds use?",["Several at once.","Exactly one.","None; they follow other birds.","Only the stars."],0],["What happens when one system is disabled?",["Another system takes over.","The birds stop flying.","They fly in circles permanently.","They lose their feathers."],0],["What makes the flight reliable?",["Redundancy among several systems.","A single brilliant instrument.","Good weather alone.","Short distances."],0]]],
        ["The idea that people have one fixed learning style, visual or auditory, is popular in schools and poorly supported by evidence. Studies that teach the same material in a student's preferred style and in another find no reliable advantage. What does help is matching the method to the material: a map is better seen, a rhythm better heard.",[["How well supported is the fixed learning-style idea?",["It is popular but poorly supported.","It is proved beyond doubt.","It has never been tested.","It applies only to adults."],0],["What did studies of preferred styles find?",["No reliable advantage for the preferred style.","A large advantage.","That styles change weekly.","That teaching is useless."],0],["What does help learning, according to the passage?",["Matching the method to the material.","Letting students choose everything.","Repeating lessons aloud.","Testing more often."],0]]],
        ["Fire has shaped some forests so thoroughly that certain pine cones open only after being heated. Decades of preventing every fire allowed dead wood to accumulate, so that when a fire finally started it burned hotter and higher than the trees could survive. Managers now set small controlled fires, an idea that still sounds alarming to visitors.",[["What do certain pine cones require?",["Heat, in order to open.","Constant shade.","Heavy rainfall.","Frozen ground."],0],["What was the result of preventing every fire?",["Dead wood accumulated and later fires burned hotter.","Forests grew faster.","Fires stopped entirely.","Trees became fireproof."],0],["What do managers now do?",["Set small controlled fires.","Remove all pines.","Water the forests.","Ban visitors."],0]]],
        ["A city's oldest buildings often survive by accident rather than admiration. Districts that were too poor to redevelop in one generation become, in the next, the only places where earlier architecture remains. Visitors then admire a street that was spared because nobody wanted it, and guidebooks describe a taste that was never exercised. Preservation arrives late and expensively, protecting what neglect had already saved.",[["Why do old buildings often survive?",["By accident, where redevelopment was unaffordable.","Because everyone admired them.","Because laws always protected them.","Because they were newly built."],0],["What happens in the following generation?",["Those districts hold the only earlier architecture left.","They are demolished first.","They become farmland.","They are forgotten entirely."],0],["How does the writer describe preservation?",["Arriving late and expensively.","Arriving early and cheaply.","Entirely unnecessary.","Always successful."],0]]],
        ["Antibiotic resistance develops because killing most bacteria leaves the survivors to multiply. Each incomplete course of treatment, each unnecessary prescription, slightly improves the odds for resistant strains. The process is ordinary evolution running at speed, and no new drug removes it; a new drug only resets the clock.",[["Why does resistance develop?",["Survivors of treatment multiply.","Bacteria learn from each other.","Drugs become weaker in storage.","Patients become immune."],0],["What improves the odds for resistant strains?",["Incomplete courses and unnecessary prescriptions.","Taking the full course.","Avoiding all medicine.","Hospital cleaning."],0],["What does a new drug achieve?",["It resets the clock without removing the process.","It ends resistance permanently.","It makes bacteria harmless.","It replaces evolution."],0]]],
        ["Subtitles were created for viewers who could not hear, and are now used by many who can. Surveys suggest the reasons are practical: unfamiliar accents, quiet dialogue mixed under loud music, and watching in places where sound would disturb others. A tool built for a minority turned out to solve problems the majority had quietly tolerated.",[["Who were subtitles originally for?",["Viewers who could not hear.","Language learners.","Film critics.","Children."],0],["What practical reasons do surveys give?",["Accents, quiet dialogue and public viewing.","Poor eyesight.","Faster reading.","Shorter films."],0],["What is the writer's general point?",["A tool for a minority solved problems many tolerated.","Subtitles are unnecessary.","Only the deaf should use them.","Sound quality has improved."],0]]],
        ["Standing in a queue feels longer than it is, and designers have learned to work with that fact rather than against it. Mirrors near lifts, winding rather than straight lines, and a visible count of positions all reduce complaints without reducing the wait by a single second. What people dislike is not time passing but time that seems unaccounted for.",[["What have designers learned?",["To work with the feeling of waiting, not only its length.","To remove all queues.","To hire more staff always.","To shorten every wait."],0],["What do mirrors and winding lines achieve?",["Fewer complaints without a shorter wait.","A faster service.","Lower costs only.","More customers."],0],["What do people actually dislike?",["Time that seems unaccounted for.","Any waiting at all.","Standing up.","Talking to strangers."],0]]],
        ["The Dead Sea is not a sea but a lake, and it is shrinking by about a metre a year. Rivers that once fed it are diverted for farming and drinking before they arrive. As the water retreats, underground salt dissolves and the ground above collapses, leaving sinkholes that appear without warning along the former shore.",[["What is the Dead Sea, strictly speaking?",["A lake.","An inland sea.","A river delta.","A reservoir."],0],["Why is it shrinking?",["Its feeding rivers are diverted before arrival.","Rainfall has doubled.","It is being pumped for salt only.","The climate has cooled."],0],["What causes the sinkholes?",["Underground salt dissolves and the ground collapses.","Earthquakes alone.","Heavy building work.","Rising sea levels."],0]]],
        ["Written examinations were introduced partly to reduce favouritism: a marker who cannot see the candidate cannot prefer a relative. The gain was real, but it came with a narrowing. Qualities that cannot be written down in two hours, such as persistence or the ability to work with others, left the record entirely.",[["Why were written examinations introduced?",["Partly to reduce favouritism.","To save paper.","To shorten the school year.","To replace teachers."],0],["What was the cost of the change?",["Qualities that cannot be written down were lost from the record.","Marking became slower.","Students wrote less.","Examinations became longer."],0],["Which qualities does the writer mention?",["Persistence and working with others.","Handwriting and spelling.","Speed and memory.","Wealth and background."],0]]],
        ["Lightning heats the air around it to roughly five times the temperature of the sun's surface. The air expands faster than sound can carry the pressure away, and the result is thunder. Counting the seconds between flash and sound gives a rough distance, because light arrives almost instantly while sound takes about three seconds to travel a kilometre.",[["How hot does lightning make the surrounding air?",["About five times the sun's surface temperature.","About the same as boiling water.","Slightly above room temperature.","Colder than ice."],0],["What causes thunder?",["Air expanding faster than sound can carry the pressure away.","Clouds colliding.","Rain striking the ground.","Wind in trees."],0],["Why does counting seconds give a distance?",["Light arrives almost instantly while sound is slow.","Sound arrives first.","Both travel at the same speed.","Thunder always lasts three seconds."],0]]],
        ["Crops that feed much of the world descend from a surprisingly narrow set of ancestors, and fields planted with genetically similar plants share the same weaknesses. Seed banks exist to hold older and wilder varieties, which may look useless until a disease arrives that the common variety cannot resist.",[["What is true of the world's main crops?",["They descend from a narrow set of ancestors.","They are unrelated to wild plants.","They change every year.","They resist all diseases."],0],["Why is genetic similarity a risk?",["Similar plants share the same weaknesses.","It lowers the yield immediately.","It makes harvesting harder.","It increases the price."],0],["What is the purpose of seed banks?",["To hold older and wilder varieties for future need.","To sell seeds cheaply.","To store grain for food.","To test fertilisers."],0]]],
        ["Early photographs required subjects to sit still for minutes, which is why so many nineteenth-century portraits look severe. The expression was not a reflection of character or of a harder age; it was the only face a person could hold for that long. Historians who read mood into these pictures are reading the limits of the equipment.",[["Why do early portraits look severe?",["Subjects had to hold still for minutes.","People were genuinely unhappier.","Photographers preferred stern faces.","The paper darkened the image."],0],["What is the mistake some historians make?",["Reading mood into a technical limitation.","Dating the photographs wrongly.","Ignoring the subjects' clothing.","Assuming the pictures are fake."],0],["What does the passage say the expression reflects?",["The equipment, not the era's character.","The subject's social class.","The photographer's instructions.","The weather on the day."],0]]],
        ["Rivers rarely run straight for long. A slight bend sends water faster along the outer bank, which erodes, while the slower inner bank collects sediment. The curve therefore deepens itself, and over centuries the river wanders across its own valley, sometimes cutting off a loop entirely and leaving a crescent lake behind.",[["What happens at the outer bank of a bend?",["Faster water erodes it.","Sediment collects there.","The water becomes still.","The bank rises."],0],["Why does a curve deepen over time?",["Erosion and deposition reinforce the bend.","Rainfall increases.","The valley tilts.","Plants hold the water back."],0],["What is left when a loop is cut off?",["A crescent-shaped lake.","A straight canal.","A waterfall.","A dry gorge."],0]]],
        ["Advertising that claims nothing specific is often the hardest to challenge. A drink described as 'refreshing' makes no promise a laboratory can test, while one claiming to contain a measured amount of vitamin C invites inspection. Regulators therefore find that the vaguest advertisements are the safest for the advertiser and the least useful for the buyer.",[["Why is vague advertising hard to challenge?",["It makes no testable promise.","It is printed too small.","It appears only on television.","It is written by lawyers."],0],["What does a measured claim invite?",["Inspection.","Higher sales.","Longer contracts.","Free samples."],0],["Who benefits from vagueness?",["The advertiser.","The buyer.","The regulator.","The laboratory."],0]]],
        ["Rice grown in flooded fields yields well but releases methane, a gas that traps heat far more effectively than carbon dioxide while remaining in the air for a shorter time. Draining the fields periodically cuts the emissions sharply without cutting the harvest much, though it demands careful timing and more labour than simply leaving the water in place.",[["What gas do flooded rice fields release?",["Methane.","Oxygen.","Nitrogen.","Hydrogen."],0],["How does this gas compare with carbon dioxide?",["It traps more heat but lasts a shorter time.","It traps less heat and lasts longer.","It is identical in effect.","It cools the air."],0],["What is the drawback of draining the fields?",["It needs careful timing and more labour.","It destroys the harvest.","It uses more water overall.","It is illegal in most countries."],0]]],
        ["The spice trade shaped more of history than its volume suggests. Pepper and cinnamon were light, durable and valuable enough to repay a journey of months, so they drew ships into oceans that carried little else worth the risk. Routes opened for spices later carried cotton, tea and ideas that the first traders never imagined.",[["Why were spices suited to long voyages?",["They were light, durable and valuable.","They were cheap and plentiful.","They could not spoil in water.","They were needed for ship repairs."],0],["What did the spice routes later carry?",["Cotton, tea and ideas.","Only spices.","Timber and stone.","Nothing further."],0],["What is the writer's main point about the spice trade?",["Its influence exceeded its volume.","Spices were overvalued.","Ships were poorly built.","Pepper was the only cargo."],0]]],
        ["Sign languages are not gestured versions of spoken ones. They have their own grammar, their own word order and their own regional varieties, and a signer of one may not understand a signer of another. They also develop spontaneously: wherever deaf children gather in numbers, a language appears within a generation whether anyone teaches it or not.",[["What is true of sign languages?",["They have their own grammar and varieties.","They copy spoken grammar exactly.","They are the same worldwide.","They have no word order."],0],["Can signers of different sign languages understand each other?",["Not necessarily.","Always, easily.","Only in writing.","Only with an interpreter present."],0],["How do these languages arise?",["Spontaneously, where deaf children gather.","Only through formal teaching.","By translating written texts.","Through government programmes."],0]]],
        ["A thermos keeps liquid hot by attacking all three routes heat takes. The vacuum between its walls blocks conduction and convection, since neither can cross empty space, while a silvered surface reflects radiation back inside. None of the three is blocked perfectly, which is why the coffee eventually cools rather than staying hot forever.",[["What does the vacuum block?",["Conduction and convection.","Radiation only.","All heat transfer completely.","Evaporation."],0],["What does the silvered surface do?",["Reflects radiation back inside.","Absorbs heat from outside.","Seals the lid.","Slows evaporation."],0],["Why does the coffee eventually cool?",["None of the three routes is blocked perfectly.","The lid is always open.","The vacuum leaks air.","Silver conducts heat well."],0]]],
        ["Committees are often blamed for slow decisions, but speed is not what they are for. A single manager decides faster and is wrong alone; a committee decides slowly and distributes both the information gathered and the responsibility carried. Organisations that complain about their committees usually want the second benefit without paying the first cost.",[["What are committees not designed for?",["Speed.","Sharing responsibility.","Gathering information.","Making decisions."],0],["What does a single manager do?",["Decide faster and be wrong alone.","Gather more information.","Share responsibility widely.","Avoid all decisions."],0],["What do complaining organisations want?",["The benefit without the cost.","Fewer decisions.","More managers.","Faster committees only."],0]]],
        ["Camels tolerate dehydration that would kill most mammals, losing up to a quarter of their body water and recovering within minutes of drinking. Their red blood cells are oval rather than round, which allows them to swell without bursting when water floods back in. The famous hump stores fat, not water, though the fat yields some water when used.",[["How much body water can a camel lose?",["Up to about a quarter.","About half.","Almost none.","Three quarters."],0],["Why are the red blood cells oval?",["So they can swell without bursting.","To carry more oxygen.","To travel faster.","To resist heat."],0],["What does the hump store?",["Fat, which yields some water when used.","Pure water.","Salt.","Air."],0]]],
        ["Some mathematical results are proved by showing that the alternative leads to nonsense. To demonstrate that a list of all prime numbers cannot be complete, one multiplies the listed primes together and adds one; the new number is divisible by none of them, so the list was never complete. The proof wins without producing a single new prime.",[["How does this kind of proof work?",["By showing the alternative leads to nonsense.","By listing every example.","By measuring carefully.","By asking experts."],0],["What is done with the listed primes?",["They are multiplied together and one is added.","They are added together.","They are divided in pairs.","They are sorted."],0],["What does the proof not do?",["Produce a new prime number.","Reach a conclusion.","Use multiplication.","Refer to the list."],0]]],
        ["The habit of eating three meals a day is recent and local rather than natural. Agricultural workers ate when work allowed; industrial shifts imposed fixed breaks, and the schedule outlived the factories that created it. Nutrition research finds no strong case for three, which does not mean the pattern is harmful, only that it is a convention.",[["How does the writer describe three meals a day?",["Recent and local rather than natural.","Ancient and universal.","Medically necessary.","Invented by doctors."],0],["What imposed fixed breaks?",["Industrial shifts.","Farming seasons.","Religious rules.","Medical advice."],0],["What does nutrition research find?",["No strong case for three specifically.","That three is clearly best.","That the pattern is harmful.","That meals should be skipped."],0]]],
        ["Glass is a liquid that has been cooled too quickly to arrange itself into a crystal, and the claim that old window panes have visibly flowed downward is false: medieval glass was simply uneven when made. Still, the underlying description holds, and physicists use it to explain why glass breaks along no particular plane while crystals split cleanly.",[["What is glass, physically speaking?",["A liquid cooled too fast to crystallise.","A soft metal.","A pressed powder.","A natural crystal."],0],["Why are old panes uneven?",["They were uneven when made.","They flowed downward over centuries.","They were damaged by sunlight.","They were cut badly."],0],["What does the description explain?",["Why glass breaks along no particular plane.","Why glass is transparent.","Why glass is cheap.","Why crystals melt slowly."],0]]],
        ["Interpreters at international meetings work in pairs and swap every twenty minutes or so, because simultaneous interpretation exhausts attention faster than almost any other mental task. The interpreter must listen, hold a sentence in memory, render it in another language, and monitor the speaker who has not stopped talking, all at once.",[["How often do interpreters swap?",["About every twenty minutes.","Once an hour.","Twice a day.","Only when tired after hours."],0],["Why do they swap so often?",["The task exhausts attention quickly.","The room is too warm.","Speakers change topic.","Equipment overheats."],0],["What must the interpreter do at once?",["Listen, remember, translate and monitor the speaker.","Only translate written text.","Speak before the speaker.","Write and then speak."],0]]],
        ["A loan at a low rate over many years can cost more in total than a loan at a high rate over a few. Borrowers who compare only the advertised rate may choose the more expensive option while believing the opposite. The figure that matters is the total paid, which lenders are often required to state but rarely display prominently.",[["What can a low rate over many years do?",["Cost more in total than a high rate over a few.","Always cost less.","Reduce the principal faster.","Remove all interest."],0],["What mistake do some borrowers make?",["Comparing only the advertised rate.","Reading the total cost.","Borrowing too little.","Repaying too early."],0],["What is said about the total paid?",["Lenders often must state it but rarely display it prominently.","It is never calculated.","It is always on the front page.","It is illegal to publish."],0]]],
        ["Volcanic ash is not soft like fireplace ash but made of tiny fragments of glass and rock with sharp edges. It scratches machinery, melts inside jet engines and collapses roofs under its weight when wet. The same material, once weathered over decades, produces some of the most fertile farmland in the world, which is why people return to the slopes.",[["What is volcanic ash made of?",["Sharp fragments of glass and rock.","Soft burnt wood.","Fine sand only.","Dried mud."],0],["What does it do inside jet engines?",["It melts.","It freezes.","It passes through harmlessly.","It cleans them."],0],["Why do people return to volcanic slopes?",["Weathered ash makes very fertile farmland.","The ash is valuable to sell.","Eruptions warn them in advance.","Land there is free."],0]]],
        ["The question 'does money make people happy' has produced an answer more precise than the question deserves. Below the level at which basic needs are met, more income raises reported wellbeing sharply; above it, the curve flattens without becoming flat. The disagreement among researchers is about where the bend lies, not whether it exists.",[["What happens below the level of basic needs?",["More income raises wellbeing sharply.","Income has no effect.","Wellbeing falls.","The curve is flat."],0],["What happens above that level?",["The curve flattens without becoming flat.","Wellbeing drops.","The effect doubles.","Income stops rising."],0],["What do researchers disagree about?",["Where the bend in the curve lies.","Whether income can be measured.","Whether happiness exists.","Whether the curve rises at all."],0]]],
        ["Birds in cities sing at higher pitches than their country relatives, and the reason appears to be traffic. Low-frequency engine noise masks low notes, so songs that carry are the ones that rise above it. Young birds learn what they can hear, and within a few generations the city population sounds measurably different.",[["How do city birds differ?",["They sing at higher pitches.","They sing more quietly.","They sing less often.","They sing only at night."],0],["What masks the low notes?",["Low-frequency engine noise.","Birdsong from other species.","Wind between buildings.","Human voices."],0],["How does the change spread?",["Young birds learn what they can hear.","Adults migrate away.","Nests are moved.","Humans train them."],0]]],
        ["Carbon dating works because living things absorb a radioactive form of carbon in a known proportion and stop absorbing it at death. Measuring how much remains gives an age, but only up to about fifty thousand years, after which too little is left to measure. Older objects need other clocks built on slower decays.",[["When do living things stop absorbing radioactive carbon?",["At death.","At birth.","When buried.","When frozen."],0],["What is the method's limit?",["About fifty thousand years.","About five hundred years.","One million years.","There is no limit."],0],["What is needed for older objects?",["Other clocks based on slower decays.","Larger samples.","Warmer storage.","Written records."],0]]],
        ["Pedestrians cross roads where it is convenient rather than where it is marked, and planners have two responses. One is to fence the convenient routes and fine those who use them; the other is to watch where the worn paths appear and put the crossing there. The second is cheaper, safer and oddly rarer.",[["Where do pedestrians cross?",["Where it is convenient, not where marked.","Only at marked crossings.","Where traffic is heaviest.","Where fences are installed."],0],["What is the second planning response?",["Put the crossing where worn paths appear.","Fence the convenient routes.","Increase the fines.","Remove the crossings."],0],["How does the writer judge the second response?",["Cheaper, safer and oddly rarer.","Expensive but popular.","Dangerous but common.","Impossible to apply."],0]]],
        ["Soap works because its molecules have two ends with opposite tastes: one clings to water, the other to oil. Trapped between them, grease is pulled off a surface and carried away in droplets that water alone could never lift. The mechanism was used for millennia before anyone could describe it.",[["What is unusual about a soap molecule?",["Its two ends attract water and oil separately.","It dissolves in neither.","It is made of pure oil.","It has no structure."],0],["What happens to the grease?",["It is pulled off and carried away in droplets.","It hardens on the surface.","It evaporates.","It sinks into the material."],0],["When was the mechanism understood?",["Long after soap was already in use.","Before soap was invented.","At the same time as its invention.","It is still unknown."],0]]],
        ["Weather forecasts have improved dramatically, yet public trust has not kept pace. Part of the reason is that a forecast of eighty per cent rain is judged wrong whenever the day stays dry, although being dry one time in five is exactly what the number promised. Probability resists the memory, which stores outcomes rather than odds.",[["What has not kept pace with better forecasts?",["Public trust.","Satellite coverage.","Computer power.","Forecast frequency."],0],["Why is an eighty per cent forecast judged wrong on a dry day?",["People expect certainty, not odds.","The forecast was miscalculated.","Eighty per cent means always.","The data was missing."],0],["What does memory store, according to the passage?",["Outcomes rather than odds.","Odds rather than outcomes.","Neither.","Exact percentages."],0]]],
        ["Most of a tree is dead. The living part is a thin layer beneath the bark that carries water upward and sugar downward, while the great mass of wood inside is structural tissue that stopped functioning long ago. This is why a hollow tree can stand and leaf for decades after its centre has rotted away.",[["Which part of a tree is alive?",["A thin layer beneath the bark.","The central wood.","The roots only.","The whole trunk."],0],["What does that layer carry?",["Water upward and sugar downward.","Only water downward.","Only air.","Nothing; it stores material."],0],["Why can a hollow tree still leaf?",["Its living layer is intact near the bark.","Its centre regrows.","Leaves need no water.","The roots replace the trunk."],0]]],
        ["The first mechanical clocks had no minute hands, because nobody needed them. Hours were enough for prayer, markets and meals. Minutes became important when work was paid by time rather than by output, and the hand was added to serve an accounting problem rather than a scientific one.",[["Why did early clocks lack minute hands?",["Hours were precise enough for daily life.","The mechanism could not turn one.","Minutes had not been invented.","They were too expensive."],0],["What made minutes important?",["Work paid by time rather than output.","Astronomical research.","Train timetables only.","Religious reform."],0],["What problem did the minute hand serve?",["An accounting problem.","A scientific one.","A navigational one.","A medical one."],0]]],
        ["Children acquire a first language without instruction and almost without error of a certain kind: they produce sentences they have never heard but almost never produce sentences in the wrong order for their language. Adults learning a second language make exactly the mistakes children avoid, which suggests the two processes are not the same thing at different ages.",[["What do children produce without instruction?",["Sentences they have never heard.","Only memorised phrases.","Written sentences.","Translations."],0],["What mistake do children almost never make?",["Putting words in the wrong order for their language.","Mispronouncing words.","Using short sentences.","Forgetting vocabulary."],0],["What does the comparison with adults suggest?",["The two processes are different, not the same at different ages.","Adults learn faster.","Children are taught secretly.","Word order is unimportant."],0]]],
        ["Flood defences can increase damage. A high wall protects the land behind it and encourages building there, so when a flood finally exceeds the wall it meets far more property than it would have met before. Engineers call this the levee effect, and it is a problem of human behaviour rather than of concrete.",[["How can flood defences increase damage?",["They encourage building in the protected area.","They weaken the ground.","They divert rivers upstream.","They collapse quickly."],0],["What happens when a flood exceeds the wall?",["It meets far more property than before.","It drains away harmlessly.","It is stopped by a second wall.","It reaches less property."],0],["What kind of problem is the levee effect?",["One of human behaviour.","One of concrete quality.","One of rainfall measurement.","One of wall height only."],0]]],
        ["A single volcanic eruption in 1815 cooled the whole planet enough to ruin harvests across Europe and North America the following year. Diaries from that summer describe snow in June and bread riots in autumn. The eruption was in Indonesia, and nobody affected by the cold connected it to a mountain they had never heard of.",[["What did the 1815 eruption do?",["It cooled the planet and ruined harvests.","It warmed Europe.","It caused floods only.","It had no measurable effect."],0],["What do diaries from that summer describe?",["Snow in June and later bread riots.","An unusually warm season.","Heavy rainfall only.","A good harvest."],0],["Why did nobody make the connection?",["The volcano was distant and unknown to them.","Scientists concealed it.","The eruption was too small.","Newspapers reported it wrongly."],0]]],
        ["Hospitals that publish their surgical success rates sometimes see those rates rise without any improvement in care, because surgeons begin refusing the most difficult cases. The measurement changes the behaviour it measures. Designers of such systems now try to adjust for how ill each patient was beforehand, which is harder than it sounds.",[["Why can published success rates rise without better care?",["Surgeons refuse the hardest cases.","Patients recover faster naturally.","Records are destroyed.","Fewer operations are counted."],0],["What general problem does this illustrate?",["Measurement changes the behaviour measured.","Surgery is unpredictable.","Publication is always harmful.","Patients choose badly."],0],["What do designers now attempt?",["Adjusting for how ill each patient was.","Hiding the figures.","Counting only easy cases.","Removing all measurement."],0]]],
        ["Wind turbines are limited by physics, not only by engineering. If a turbine extracted all the energy from the air passing through it, the air would stop, and stopped air cannot make way for more. The theoretical maximum is therefore under sixty per cent, and good modern machines reach most of that.",[["Why can a turbine not extract all the energy?",["Stopped air cannot make way for more.","The blades would melt.","Wind is too weak.","The gears would break."],0],["What is the theoretical maximum?",["Under sixty per cent.","One hundred per cent.","About ten per cent.","Exactly ninety per cent."],0],["How do good modern machines compare?",["They reach most of the maximum.","They exceed it.","They reach only a tenth of it.","They cannot be measured."],0]]],
        ["Written contracts are often less important than the relationship around them. Studies of long-standing suppliers find that disputes are usually settled by negotiation, with the contract consulted rarely and enforced in court almost never. The document matters most as a record of what was intended and as a warning of what the parties could do if trust failed.",[["How are most disputes between long-standing suppliers settled?",["By negotiation.","In court.","By arbitration panels.","By ending the relationship."],0],["How often is the contract enforced in court?",["Almost never.","In most disputes.","Every year.","Whenever consulted."],0],["What does the document mainly provide?",["A record of intention and a warning.","A detailed price list.","Legal immunity.","A substitute for trust."],0]]],
        ["The deepest part of the ocean is under pressure more than a thousand times that at the surface, and yet it is not empty. Creatures there live on material that drifts down from above, including the occasional whale carcass that feeds a whole community for years. Light never reaches them, and some make their own.",[["What is the pressure at the deepest point?",["More than a thousand times surface pressure.","About twice surface pressure.","Lower than at the surface.","The same as at the surface."],0],["What do deep-sea creatures live on?",["Material drifting down from above.","Plants growing on the seabed.","Sunlight filtered through water.","Fresh water from rivers."],0],["What does the passage say about light there?",["It never reaches them; some make their own.","It reaches them faintly.","It reaches them only at noon.","They have no use for it."],0]]],
        ["Universities began as guilds of students or teachers who bargained with the towns that housed them. When a town raised rents too far, the scholars could and did leave, taking their spending with them. Several famous institutions exist because an earlier one was abandoned in a dispute, which is a founding story rarely told on open days.",[["What were early universities?",["Guilds that bargained with their host towns.","Royal military academies.","Monasteries for silent study.","Private family schools."],0],["What did scholars do when rents rose too far?",["They left, taking their spending away.","They petitioned the king.","They paid and stayed.","They closed the town gates."],0],["How did several famous institutions begin?",["An earlier one was abandoned in a dispute.","A royal gift of land.","A merger of two schools.","A religious vision."],0]]],
        ["Sand is the second most used resource on earth after water, and the desert kind is largely useless for concrete: wind-rounded grains do not lock together. Construction needs the angular sand found in riverbeds and coasts, which is why some countries sit on vast dunes and import sand to build.",[["Where does sand rank among used resources?",["Second, after water.","First overall.","Tenth.","It is not measured."],0],["Why is desert sand poor for concrete?",["Wind-rounded grains do not lock together.","It contains too much salt.","It is too heavy.","It dissolves in water."],0],["What follows from this for some countries?",["They import sand despite having dunes.","They export desert sand widely.","They build only with stone.","They stopped constructing."],0]]],
        ["Honeybees decide where to build a new nest by a process that resembles voting. Scouts inspect sites and advertise them by dancing, with the vigour of the dance reflecting the quality of what was found. Other scouts check the better advertisements themselves rather than taking the dancer's word, and a decision emerges once enough agree.",[["How do scouts advertise a site?",["By dancing with vigour matching its quality.","By leading others there silently.","By leaving scent marks only.","By fighting rival scouts."],0],["What do other scouts do with a good advertisement?",["They inspect the site themselves.","They accept it immediately.","They ignore it.","They repeat the dance without looking."],0],["When is the decision reached?",["Once enough scouts agree.","When the queen chooses.","After a fixed number of days.","When the first scout returns."],0]]],
        ["Fingerprint evidence was accepted in courts long before anyone established how often two prints might resemble each other closely enough to confuse an examiner. The identification is made by a trained human comparing patterns, and studies that gave the same prints to different examiners found disagreement more often than the technique's reputation suggested.",[["What was not established before courts accepted fingerprints?",["How often two prints might be confused.","Whether fingers have patterns.","How to photograph prints.","Whether prints change with age."],0],["Who makes the identification?",["A trained human comparing patterns.","A machine, automatically.","The investigating officer.","The accused person."],0],["What did studies with different examiners find?",["Disagreement more often than expected.","Perfect agreement.","That prints are identical.","That examiners refuse to judge."],0]]],
        ["A tax on something people can easily stop buying raises little money and changes a lot of behaviour; a tax on something they cannot avoid raises a lot of money and changes little. Governments wanting revenue and governments wanting change therefore look for opposite properties, and a tax praised for one purpose often fails at the other.",[["What does a tax on an avoidable purchase do?",["Raises little money but changes behaviour.","Raises much money and changes nothing.","Raises much money and changes behaviour.","Has no effect at all."],0],["What do revenue-seeking governments want?",["Something people cannot easily avoid.","Something easily given up.","A tax nobody notices.","No tax at all."],0],["What conclusion does the writer draw about taxes?",["A tax good for one purpose often fails the other.","All taxes work equally.","Taxes should be abolished.","Behaviour never changes."],0]]],
        ["Plastic in the ocean breaks into fragments rather than disappearing, and the smallest pieces are now found in rain, in soil and inside animals at every level of the food chain. Cleaning the surface removes the visible share, which is a small fraction of the total. The rest is dispersed too finely for any net.",[["What happens to ocean plastic over time?",["It breaks into smaller fragments.","It dissolves completely.","It sinks and stays whole.","It evaporates."],0],["Where are the smallest pieces found?",["In rain, soil and animals throughout the food chain.","Only on beaches.","Only in deep water.","Nowhere measurable."],0],["What is the limit of surface cleaning?",["It removes only a small visible fraction.","It removes everything.","It is forbidden.","It costs nothing."],0]]],
        ["Snow is a good insulator because most of its volume is trapped air. Animals that burrow beneath it find temperatures far milder than the surface, and farmers once valued a deep covering for protecting winter wheat. A cold winter without snow can therefore damage crops more than a colder winter with it.",[["Why does snow insulate well?",["Most of its volume is trapped air.","It is denser than soil.","It reflects sunlight.","It conducts heat quickly."],0],["What do burrowing animals find beneath it?",["Temperatures far milder than the surface.","Colder temperatures.","No difference at all.","Standing water."],0],["What can a cold winter without snow do?",["Damage crops more than a colder snowy one.","Improve the harvest.","Have no effect on wheat.","Warm the soil."],0]]],
        ["Memory does not replay events; it rebuilds them each time, borrowing details from later knowledge. This is why confident witnesses can be wrong about things they clearly remember, and why asking a leading question can change what a person recalls on the second telling. Confidence measures how smoothly the rebuilding went, not how accurate it was.",[["How does memory work, according to the passage?",["It rebuilds events, borrowing later details.","It replays events exactly.","It stores events permanently unchanged.","It records only emotions."],0],["What can a leading question do?",["Change what a person recalls afterwards.","Improve accuracy.","Erase the memory.","Have no effect."],0],["What does confidence measure?",["How smoothly the rebuilding went.","How accurate the memory is.","How long ago the event was.","How important the event was."],0]]],
        ["Ports have moved away from city centres almost everywhere, pushed by the container. Loading a ship once needed hundreds of workers and many days; now it needs cranes, acres of flat land and a deep channel, none of which old waterfronts could supply. The warehouses they left behind became apartments, which is a kind of accidental planning.",[["What pushed ports out of city centres?",["The shipping container.","Rising sea levels.","A shortage of workers only.","Government decree alone."],0],["What does modern loading require?",["Cranes, flat land and a deep channel.","Hundreds of workers.","Many days in port.","Narrow docks."],0],["What became of the old warehouses?",["They became apartments.","They were all demolished.","They remained empty forever.","They were moved elsewhere."],0]]],
        ["Standardised time zones were created for railways, not for people. Before them each town kept its own noon, which was harmless until trains made the difference between two towns' clocks a matter of collisions. The sun's actual position was sacrificed to the timetable, and nobody has seriously proposed reversing the trade.",[["Why were time zones created?",["For railways.","For farmers.","For astronomers.","For radio broadcasting."],0],["What did towns keep before them?",["Their own local noon.","A single national clock.","No clocks at all.","The railway's time."],0],["What was sacrificed?",["The sun's actual position.","The railway timetable.","Local government.","Accurate clocks."],0]]],
        ["Research papers reporting a positive result are published far more often than those reporting none, even when the second kind is equally well conducted. The literature therefore exaggerates how often effects are real. Journals that have begun accepting studies before the results are known are trying to correct a bias built into the record itself.",[["Which papers are published more often?",["Those reporting a positive result.","Those reporting no effect.","Those with the largest samples.","Those written by students."],0],["What is the consequence for the literature?",["It exaggerates how often effects are real.","It is perfectly balanced.","It contains too few studies.","It favours negative findings."],0],["What are some journals now doing?",["Accepting studies before results are known.","Publishing fewer papers.","Rejecting all positive findings.","Charging higher fees."],0]]],
        ["Elephants recognise individual human voices and distinguish languages spoken by groups that have historically hunted them from those that have not. They respond with defensive behaviour to one and ignore the other. The knowledge is held by older females and passed to the herd, which makes the loss of an old matriarch more than one animal's death.",[["What can elephants distinguish?",["Languages of groups that hunted them from those that did not.","Only the volume of a voice.","Written words.","Nothing about humans."],0],["How do they respond to the threatening group?",["With defensive behaviour.","By approaching curiously.","By ignoring it.","By calling louder."],0],["Why does losing an old matriarch matter especially?",["She holds knowledge passed to the herd.","She is the largest animal.","She leads the migration route only.","She protects the water source."],0]]],
        ["Chess computers defeated the best human players decades ago, and the game did not die. Human tournaments continue, attendance has risen, and players study using the machines that beat them. The example is quoted whenever a new technology is said to make a human activity pointless, though quoting it does not prove the next case will go the same way.",[["What happened to chess after computers won?",["Human tournaments continued and attendance rose.","The game was abandoned.","Humans stopped studying.","Computers were banned."],0],["How do players use the machines now?",["They study with them.","They refuse to touch them.","They sell them.","They compete against them only."],0],["What caution does the writer add?",["The example does not prove the next case will match.","The example is false.","Chess is unimportant.","Computers will always lose."],0]]],
        ["Insects dominate the land by number and by variety, and one reason is small size: a creature a few millimetres long can live its whole life in a space another species would overlook, so a single tree may hold dozens of species that never meet. Large animals need large territories and are therefore always fewer.",[["What advantage does small size give insects?",["A tiny space can hold a whole life.","Greater strength.","Longer lifespans.","Better eyesight."],0],["What may a single tree hold?",["Dozens of species that never meet.","Only one species.","No insects at all.","A single large animal."],0],["Why are large animals fewer?",["They need large territories.","They reproduce faster.","They cannot climb.","They avoid trees."],0]]],
        ["Signatures are a poor security measure and survive because they are familiar. They are easy to copy, rarely checked carefully, and vary from day to day even for the honest signer. Banks have long known this, which is why the signature on a card matters less than the systems watching the transaction behind it.",[["Why do signatures survive?",["They are familiar.","They cannot be copied.","They are checked rigorously.","They never vary."],0],["What is true even for honest signers?",["Their signature varies from day to day.","It is identical every time.","It cannot be read.","It is never required."],0],["What matters more than the signature?",["The systems watching the transaction.","The colour of the ink.","The size of the card.","The signer's name."],0]]],
        ["Oases are fed by water that fell as rain thousands of kilometres away and thousands of years ago, travelling slowly through rock. Pumping faster than that supply arrives drains a store nobody alive will see refilled. The water is better described as a mineral being mined than as a crop being harvested.",[["Where does oasis water come from?",["Rain that fell far away and long ago.","Recent local rainfall.","Melting snow nearby.","Sea water filtered by sand."],0],["What happens if pumping outpaces supply?",["A store is drained that will not refill in a lifetime.","The water returns quickly.","Rainfall increases.","The oasis spreads."],0],["How does the writer prefer to describe the water?",["As a mineral being mined.","As a crop being harvested.","As an endless resource.","As rainfall in storage."],0]]],
        ["Sleep deprivation affects judgement before it affects feeling, which is the dangerous part. People who have slept five hours for several nights perform measurably worse on attention tasks while rating their own alertness as normal. The faculty needed to notice the impairment is itself impaired, so self-report cannot be trusted here.",[["What does sleep deprivation affect first?",["Judgement, before feeling.","Feeling, before judgement.","Only physical strength.","Nothing measurable."],0],["How do the sleep-deprived rate their own alertness?",["As normal, despite worse performance.","As much worse than normal.","They refuse to rate it.","Accurately."],0],["Why can self-report not be trusted?",["The faculty needed to notice is itself impaired.","People deliberately lie.","The tests are unreliable.","Sleep cannot be measured."],0]]],
        ["Paper money began as a receipt. Merchants deposited coin with a trusted holder and carried a written claim instead, which was lighter and safer on the road. The claim circulated because people accepted it as equal to the coin, and the step from receipt to currency was taken long before any government declared it.",[["What was paper money originally?",["A receipt for deposited coin.","A government decree.","A tax record.","A printed ornament."],0],["Why was the written claim preferred on the road?",["It was lighter and safer.","It earned interest.","It was more decorative.","It could not be stolen."],0],["When was the step to currency taken?",["Before any government declared it.","Only after a royal decree.","In the twentieth century.","It never happened."],0]]],
        ["A species introduced where it has no predators can multiply without the checks it faced at home. Rabbits in Australia and water hyacinth in African lakes both spread at rates their native ranges never allowed. Removing them afterwards costs far more than preventing the arrival would have, a calculation made easily in hindsight.",[["What is missing for an introduced species?",["The predators that checked it at home.","Enough food.","Suitable climate.","Space to live."],0],["What examples does the writer give?",["Rabbits in Australia and water hyacinth in African lakes.","Camels in Arabia and olives in Spain.","Wheat in Egypt.","Horses in Mongolia."],0],["How do removal and prevention compare in cost?",["Removal costs far more.","Prevention costs more.","They cost the same.","Neither has a cost."],0]]],
        ["Earthquakes kill through buildings rather than through shaking. Two cities can experience the same ground motion and record death tolls differing by a factor of a hundred, the difference lying in whether walls were tied to floors and whether anyone inspected the work. The engineering is well understood; the enforcement is the hard part.",[["How do earthquakes mainly kill?",["Through buildings rather than shaking itself.","Through fire only.","Through flooding.","Through gas escapes."],0],["What explains very different death tolls?",["Construction quality and inspection.","The depth of the earthquake.","The time of year.","Population age."],0],["What does the writer call the hard part?",["Enforcement.","The engineering.","Predicting earthquakes.","Measuring ground motion."],0]]],
        ["Colour names differ between languages in ways that are not arbitrary. Languages with few terms almost always divide the spectrum at the same places, beginning with dark and light, then red, then green or yellow. The order is consistent enough that a language's colour vocabulary can be predicted from its size.",[["How do languages with few colour terms divide the spectrum?",["At almost the same places.","Randomly.","By climate.","By the speakers' occupations."],0],["Which terms come first?",["Dark and light.","Green and blue.","Red and purple.","Yellow and grey."],0],["What can be predicted from the vocabulary's size?",["Which colour terms a language has.","How many speakers it has.","Its grammar.","Its age."],0]]],
        ["Recycling glass saves energy because melting old glass needs a lower temperature than making new glass from sand. Recycling aluminium saves far more, since extracting the metal from ore is one of the most electricity-hungry industrial processes there is. Not all recycling is equally worthwhile, and treating it as a single virtue hides that.",[["Why does recycling glass save energy?",["Melting old glass needs a lower temperature.","Glass cannot be made from sand.","Old glass is lighter.","Sand is unavailable."],0],["Why does aluminium recycling save more?",["Extracting it from ore uses enormous electricity.","Aluminium is rarer.","It melts at room temperature.","It is heavier than glass."],0],["What does the writer warn against?",["Treating all recycling as equally worthwhile.","Recycling at all.","Using glass containers.","Measuring energy use."],0]]],
        ["Markets for second-hand goods depend on trust that the seller knows more than the buyer. A seller aware of a hidden fault will sell at the honest price and keep the sound item, so buyers lower what they offer, so honest sellers withdraw. Warranties and inspections exist to interrupt this spiral rather than to reassure the nervous.",[["What does the seller typically know?",["More than the buyer about the item.","Less than the buyer.","Exactly the same.","Nothing about the item."],0],["What do buyers do in response?",["Lower what they offer.","Pay more to be safe.","Stop inspecting.","Buy only new goods."],0],["What is the purpose of warranties?",["To interrupt the spiral of distrust.","To reassure nervous buyers only.","To raise prices.","To delay sales."],0]]],
        ["Lighthouses were long used as the standard example of a service no private party would provide, since a ship cannot be charged for seeing a light. Historians later found that several were in fact built and run privately, funded by fees collected at the ports the ships were heading for. The example survived in textbooks longer than in fact.",[["Why were lighthouses used as an example?",["A ship cannot be charged for seeing the light.","They cost nothing to build.","Only governments could build them.","Ships refused to pay taxes."],0],["What did historians find?",["Several were built and run privately.","All were state-owned.","None existed before 1900.","Ships paid the keeper directly."],0],["How were the private ones funded?",["By fees collected at destination ports.","By donations from sailors.","By selling the light's oil.","By royal grants only."],0]]],
        ["The human eye sees detail only in a small central patch; the rest of the visual field is coarse and mostly colour-blind. The impression of a sharp, complete scene is assembled from rapid movements that sample it several times a second. What feels like looking is in fact a summary, and the summary is usually right.",[["Where does the eye see detail?",["In a small central patch.","Across the whole field.","At the edges.","Only in bright light."],0],["How is the impression of a complete scene produced?",["By rapid sampling movements several times a second.","By holding the eye still.","By combining both eyes only.","By memory alone."],0],["What does the writer say about the summary?",["It feels like looking and is usually right.","It is usually wrong.","It is identical to the real scene.","It is produced by one glance."],0]]],
        ["Mangrove forests grow where salt water meets land, and their tangled roots hold sediment that would otherwise wash away. Coasts stripped of mangroves for shrimp ponds or hotels lose both the land and the fish nurseries the roots shelter. Replanting is possible but slow, and a young mangrove protects very little.",[["Where do mangroves grow?",["Where salt water meets land.","High in mountains.","In fresh water only.","In deserts."],0],["What do their roots do?",["Hold sediment that would wash away.","Filter drinking water.","Produce fruit for export.","Absorb all rainfall."],0],["What is the limit of replanting?",["It is slow and young trees protect little.","It is impossible.","It is instant.","It requires salt removal."],0]]],
        ["Expert intuition is reliable in some fields and worthless in others, and the difference is feedback. A nurse who sees hundreds of patients and learns within hours which ones deteriorated develops genuine pattern recognition. A forecaster whose predictions concern events five years away receives no such correction and may grow confident without growing accurate.",[["What distinguishes reliable intuition from worthless intuition?",["The presence of feedback.","Years of experience alone.","Formal qualifications.","Natural talent."],0],["Why does the nurse's intuition improve?",["Outcomes are learned within hours.","Patients are all similar.","Training is longer.","Records are detailed."],0],["What happens to the long-range forecaster?",["Confidence grows without accuracy.","Accuracy grows steadily.","Both improve together.","Neither changes."],0]]],
        ["The printing press did not immediately produce cheap books. For decades printers imitated manuscripts closely, using the same scripts and leaving space for hand-painted initials, and prices fell only when they stopped pretending. New technologies often spend their early years copying what they replace, and the real change arrives when someone designs for the new method itself.",[["What did early printers imitate?",["Manuscripts, including their scripts and painted initials.","Newspapers.","Stone inscriptions.","Each other's prices."],0],["When did prices fall?",["When printers stopped imitating manuscripts.","Immediately after the invention.","After governments intervened.","When paper became scarce."],0],["What general pattern does the writer describe?",["New technologies first copy what they replace.","New technologies change everything at once.","Old methods always survive.","Imitation is always profitable."],0]]],
        ["Trees in a forest are connected underground by fungal networks that carry water, nutrients and chemical signals between roots. A large tree can pass sugar to a shaded seedling of the same species through this web. Whether the arrangement is cooperation or exploitation by the fungus is still argued, which is a reminder that a striking finding and its interpretation are separate things.",[["What connects trees underground?",["Fungal networks.","Shared root tips.","Insect tunnels.","Underground streams."],0],["What can pass through the network?",["Water, nutrients and chemical signals.","Only water.","Only insects.","Nothing measurable."],0],["What is still argued about?",["Whether it is cooperation or exploitation.","Whether the networks exist.","Whether trees have roots.","Whether fungi are alive."],0]]],
        ["Crowds move more like fluids than like collections of individuals, and the dangerous moment is not panic but density. When people are packed closely enough that a small push travels through the crowd as a wave, no one can control their own footing however calm they remain. Good event design therefore manages how many bodies occupy a space, not how they feel.",[["What do crowds resemble in their movement?",["Fluids.","Marching soldiers.","Flocks of birds only.","Rigid structures."],0],["What is the dangerous factor?",["Density.","Panic.","Noise.","Darkness."],0],["What should event design manage?",["How many bodies occupy a space.","How calm people feel.","The quality of announcements.","The number of exits only."],0]]]
      ];
})();
